WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.225
Each extinguishing agent suits certain fires, so match the agent to the class.

2
00:00:05.475 --> 00:00:10.300
Dry chemical, rated A B C or B C, is the one on most boats.

3
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.800
It works on nearly everything.

4
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.500
The price is a corrosive powder all over your boat.

5
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:22.625
Carbon dioxide works on fuel and electrical fires by displacing the oxygen.

6
00:00:22.875 --> 00:00:24.775
It leaves no mess.

7
00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:29.725
But it has short range, and in a closed space it can suffocate you too.

8
00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:34.875
Clean agents are what you’ll usually find in a fixed engine-room system.

9
00:00:35.125 --> 00:00:38.900
Foam smothers fuel fires by floating on top of them.

10
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:40.625
And water.

11
00:00:40.875 --> 00:00:43.425
It’s excellent on Class A fires.

12
00:00:43.675 --> 00:00:48.450
But what happens if you hit burning gasoline with a solid stream of water?

13
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.075
You spread it.

14
00:00:51.325 --> 00:00:56.200
And on live electrical gear, water conducts the current right back to you.

15
00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.025
So water is for Class A only.
